/target
**/*.rs.bk
__pycache__/

# `.claude/` is deliberately NOT ignored wholesale any more.
#
# It was, on the reasoning that none of it is project content. But five files
# under it had to be force-added anyway -- the review skill, its CATCHUP note,
# and three workflow harnesses -- precisely because they are, so the exception
# was re-argued every time somebody added one, and anything nobody thought to
# force-add simply vanished. Two things settled the reversal:
#
#   1. **Most development now happens in cloud containers that are destroyed
#      after the session.** Config that is not committed does not merely live
#      on one machine -- for those sessions it does not exist at all. A
#      per-machine `settings.json` helps nobody in a container that starts from
#      a fresh clone, which is exactly where the sessions most needing a shared
#      baseline are running.
#   2. Claude Code's own split is `settings.json` shared, `settings.local.json`
#      per-machine. The blanket rule inverted it.
#
# So: name the machine-local things, and let project content -- skills,
# workflows, hooks, agents, commands, the shared settings -- be tracked like
# any other source.
#
# `worktrees/` is the real hazard the blanket rule existed to stop and is still
# ignored: it holds entire checkouts of this repo, and one swept into a commit
# would carry another session's in-progress tree with it. Anything else that is
# genuinely machine-local gets its own line here rather than a new blanket.
/.claude/worktrees/
/.claude/settings.local.json
/.claude/*.local.json
/.claude/**/*.local.json

# scripts/branchcheck.sh --selftest writes a mutated copy of itself here to
# prove its own check can go red, and removes it in an EXIT trap. Ignored so
# that a selftest killed mid-run cannot leak the mutant into a commit.
scripts/.branchcheck-selftest-mutant.sh

# The specimen shelf: jars a player keeps while working, written by the lab
# at runtime (`sim::specimen`). Ignored deliberately -- these are one player's
# working set, not project content, and the shelf never overwrites so a shared
# one would only ever grow. Promoting a jar into `assets/species/` is what
# makes a specimen part of the game, and that lands as a tracked file.
/assets/shelf/

# The lab's own session state: the bed spec (`lab::scene::LabBox::save`) and
# the world-level rule/heredity dials (`lab::params::Dials::save`) a player
# saved from the parameters page. Same rationale as the shelf above -- one
# player's current box, not authored content -- and unlike `assets/player.ron`
# or `assets/explosion.ron`, which are the shipped game's own tuning and are
# meant to be committed.
/assets/lab_bed.ron
/assets/lab_dials.ron

# Contact sheets the `examples/` harnesses write to whatever directory they
# are run from, which is normally the repo root. They are output to be looked
# at and thrown away -- `labui` alone is 5 MB a run -- and every PNG that is
# genuinely tracked here lives under `docs/screenshots/` or `Reports/img/`,
# put there deliberately. Named individually rather than as a blanket `*.png`
# so that curating a sheet into either of those directories still works
# without a force-add.
/filmstrip.png
/lab_resolution.png
/labshot.png
/labui.png
/labzoom.png
/uishot.png
/vision_probe.png
/labdial.gif
